{% lede %}
A template is only as good as what other people make with it. I built a Caliper & Crayon brand kit and presentation template in Canva, carrying over the type and color system from this site, and designed it so someone who isn't a designer can make an on-brand deck without breaking it.
{% /lede %}

## Decisions

- **One type system, two jobs.** Bricolage Grotesque for titles, headings and body; DM Mono in spaced capitals for labels and captions. The same pairing runs this website, so a deck and the site read as one brand.
- **Black and white do the work.** Ink on white carries everything. Blue appears once per slide at most, for the one thing that matters, so it never turns into decoration.
- **Placeholders that teach.** Instead of lorem ipsum, the empty template says what goes there: "Heading, under six words," "Figure 1 · Caption goes here." The instructions are the guardrails.
- **Lock what shouldn't move.** The logo, section labels and image frames are locked in place; only the text and pictures are editable. Every slide shares the same margins, so content lands in the same place every time.
- **A slide for figures.** Most templates stop at title and bullets. The third slide is one large image with a caption, because that's what scientists actually present.
